{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1794341597,"nonce":"LlQIgm21yNACRls6pAVuc4jDMjA=","title":"Core shader draws a Circle primitive's border only at four points","message":"A `PrimitiveType::Circle` primitive with a border (what `stroke_circle_impl` emits) gets its border ring from `sdf_core` only where the circle touches its bounding square. The border block takes a fast path for fragments inside the bounding rect inset by the border width, and the rect is what it measures: a circle primitive has `corner_radius` zero, so every fragment on the arc between the four extremes is \"within the inner straight\" and skips the border.\n\nReproducer: draw `GpuPrimitive::circle(94.0, 108.0, 14.0).with_border(2.0, 0.0, 0.0, 0.0, 1.0)` with `sdf_core` and with the compact `sdf_simple`: the compact one has the black ring all round, core only at the extremes.\n\nWho sees it: tiers without vertex storage (vertex buffer and data texture) have no compact pipeline, so stroked circles there draw wrongly. Tiers with vertex storage send circles to the compact shader, which is right.\n\nFix direction: for a circle primitive treat the corner radius as half the short side before the fast-path test (or skip the fast path for circles). Then add a bordered circle back to `plain_scene` in `russl_parity.rs`, which leaves it out for now.","files":null}]}